Spawn, objective 167 [recoverability] (projects-47, 2026-11-14; moved from rev-b to rev-e; reviewer rev-g): two `spawn backup bundle` runs in one second no longer collide.

Why: a bundle's object name has one-second resolution (`bundles/<tier>/spawn-bundle-YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS.<ext>.gpg`). A second bundle in the same UTC second names the same object. The bucket's lock refuses the PUT (409 ObjectLockedByBucketPolicy, R2's and the stand-in's answer), and the bundle exits 1. rev-b found it behind 162.sh's flake (3 of 5 runs; rev-b-tmp/proof/kept-162-*.out). Any scenario making bundles back to back may hit it (154.sh (6), 162.sh). On the host a second run in one second is a by-hand bundle beside the timer's.

Read first: crates/spawn-cli/src/backup.rs (`bundle`: the key; `Stamp::at`, `Stamp::parse`; `random_hex`; `bundle_stamps`); crates/spawn-cli/src/backup/store.rs (`manifest`: `{stamp}-{random}`, the precedent).

What changes (the candidate, 167-fleet.diff, crates/spawn-cli/src/backup.rs only):
- `bundle_name(tier, now_ms, extension, random)`: `bundles/<tier>/spawn-bundle-YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS-mmm-<random>.<extension>.gpg`. It holds the UTC second, the millisecond in three digits, and 4 bytes of /dev/urandom as 8 hex digits. `bundle` stores under `settings.stored(&bundle_name(tier, now_ms, extension, &random_hex(4)?))`.
- The names sort, by byte, as the bundles ran: the second first, then the fixed-width millisecond. The random part only separates two bundles of one millisecond.
- `Stamp::parse` still reads the second from a name (the first YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS in it), so the full/increment decision reads the week as before. Old one-second names stay readable.

Readers of the names, each checked:
- the restore chain (restore_drill.rs `chain_of`) goes by event ids and base_event_id, never by name;
- `list_bucket` only counts the bytes held;
- no code thins or prunes. Expiry and locks are R2's rules by prefix and age (r2-lifecycle-rules.json, r2-lock-rules.json), which the name does not touch;
- `bundle_stamps` and `Stamp::parse`: unchanged results (tested);
- deploy/backup/lib.sh's R2_KEY_RE and check-j12-hold-told.sh's regexes are dump names (postgres/...), unchanged;
- scenarios that read bundle keys take them from the records (104, 149, 154, 162), and fixtures with made-up names are not produced by `bundle`.
In the bucket, an old one-second name sorts after a new name of the same second ('.' > '-'). This happens only at the change itself, and no reader sorts names.

What stays as it is: dump names (`postgres/<tier>/spawn-YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS.dump.gpg`: one dump an hour under the lock, and lib.sh's regex reads them); records; the restore and the drill.

Owner change (167-owner.diff, deploy/backup/README.md, protected): the layout line and the decrypt example name the new form, and say why.

Tests: crates/spawn-cli/src/backup.rs `bundles_in_one_second_get_names_that_sort_as_they_ran`. It checks:
- the exact form;
- two bundles in one second named apart, and in one millisecond named apart by the random part;
- names over times that cross millisecond widths, seconds, an hour and a day sort as their times, with their random parts descending;
- an earlier second's one-second name sorts first;
- the stamp is read from the name, under a prefix too.

Files: crates/spawn-cli/src/backup.rs (candidate); deploy/backup/README.md and rsi/scenarios/167.sh (owner). Size: +79 -9, the test included (code +16 -5).

Acceptance: rsi/scenarios/167.sh, black box, on its own database, scratch HOME and GNUPGHOME, against 104's R2 stand-in. Bundles are made back to back, at most 40, until two of them fall in one UTC second twice. Then:
(1) every bundle exits 0 and the stand-in refused no PUT;
(2) each record names one key, every key is new, and each is an object;
(3) the keys sort, by byte, in run order;
(4) a restore drill over the whole chain passes and GETs every key.
No same-second pair in 40 runs is a harness failure (2): under heavy load (bundles over about 1 s) the gate sees exit 2, never a false pass (rev-g's n1, R2 text only). On the base (80debd0f) it exits 1 at (1): the 409.

Proof: 167-proof.txt (rev-e-96). R1 PASS by rev-g (sync/review-167-bundle-names.md); R2 adds one sentence to Acceptance, text only.

Verified by: after the landing, rsi/scenarios/167.sh in the gate; on live, the next bundles' keys in their backup.bundled records carry the new form, and `spawn backup restore-drill` (by hand, as 149 holds) reads them. Then 162.sh's one-second wait (last_bundle_s) may go.
